From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 02:41:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 345/353] PCI: apple: tell the PCI core a restarted T8132 port
 lost power

After a T8132 deep sleep the APCIe block comes back with its root ports
down, and with t8132_port_restart the host sets each one up again in
its noirq resume. Everything behind the port lost power with it, but the
PCI core still has the root port and its endpoints in D0 or D3hot, the
states it left them in. So it restores their configuration space at
once, without waiting for them to answer: the PCI core runs
pci_bridge_wait_for_secondary_bus() on resume only for a bridge that was
in D3cold. An endpoint not
ready yet then reads all ones ("device inaccessible"), the restore goes
nowhere, and its driver resumes against unprogrammed BARs.

For a port the sleep left down, record D3cold for the root port and
every device below it before the PCI core resumes them. From D3cold the
core waits the port's delay, polls each endpoint until it answers (up to
PCI_RESET_WAIT), restores it, and marks the devices disconnected if the
link never came back.

Nothing changes for a sleep that keeps power, nor without a T8132
deep sleep.

Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Assisted-by: LLM
---
 drivers/pci/controller/pcie-apple.c | 32 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 32 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/pci/controller/pcie-apple.c b/drivers/pci/controller/pcie-apple.c
index af477027a6..a26fcb673a 100644
--- a/drivers/pci/controller/pcie-apple.c
+++ b/drivers/pci/controller/pcie-apple.c
@@ -5231,6 +5231,37 @@ static int apple_pcie_t8132_port_setup(struct apple_pcie *pcie, struct apple_pci
 	return 0;
 }
 
+static int apple_pcie_t8132_mark_d3cold(struct pci_dev *pdev, void *unused)
+{
+	pdev->current_state = PCI_D3cold;
+	return 0;
+}
+
+/*
+ * A port the sleep left down lost power with everything behind it, while
+ * the PCI core still has its root port and endpoints in D0 or D3hot. Record
+ * D3cold for them, which is what happened: from D3cold the core waits for
+ * the endpoints to answer (pci_bridge_wait_for_secondary_bus()) before it
+ * restores their configuration space, and treats them as disconnected if
+ * they never do, instead of restoring into a device that is not ready yet.
+ */
+static void apple_pcie_t8132_power_lost(struct apple_pcie *pcie,
+					struct apple_pcie_port *port)
+{
+	struct pci_host_bridge *bridge = pci_host_bridge_from_priv(pcie);
+	struct pci_dev *rp;
+
+	if (!bridge->bus)
+		return;
+	rp = pci_get_slot(bridge->bus, PCI_DEVFN(port->idx, 0));
+	if (!rp)
+		return;
+	rp->current_state = PCI_D3cold;
+	if (rp->subordinate)
+		pci_walk_bus(rp->subordinate, apple_pcie_t8132_mark_d3cold, NULL);
+	pci_dev_put(rp);
+}
+
 static void apple_pcie_t8132_restart(struct apple_pcie *pcie)
 {
 	struct apple_pcie_port *port;
@@ -5245,6 +5276,7 @@ static void apple_pcie_t8132_restart(struct apple_pcie *pcie)
 		down = apple_sleep_apcie_port_down(port->idx);
 		if (!down)
 			continue;
+		apple_pcie_t8132_power_lost(pcie, port);
 		if (down < 0) {
 			WRITE_ONCE(port->link_failed, true);
 			dev_err(pcie->dev, "port %pOF: its APCIe block stayed off after the sleep: %d\n",
-- 
